WEBVTT

00:03.800 --> 00:04.160
All right.

00:04.200 --> 00:06.480
Now we have another use case for the set operators.

00:06.480 --> 00:08.280
It's more for data engineers.

00:08.280 --> 00:13.320
We can use the accept in order to find the delta between two batches of data.

00:13.320 --> 00:20.400
For example, data engineers build data pipelines in order to load daily new data from the source systems

00:20.400 --> 00:22.520
to data warehouse or a data lake.

00:22.520 --> 00:28.400
Now, in those data pipelines, we have to build a logic in order to identify what are the new data

00:28.400 --> 00:32.960
that is generated from the source system, in order to insert it in the data warehouse.

00:32.960 --> 00:39.360
One way to do it is to use the set operator, except in order to compare the current data with the previous

00:39.360 --> 00:39.720
load.

00:39.720 --> 00:41.120
Let's have a very simple example.

00:41.120 --> 00:41.560
In the day.

00:41.600 --> 00:44.120
Number one, we have two customers one and two.

00:44.160 --> 00:45.280
So what's going to happen in this day?

00:45.280 --> 00:48.640
We're going to go and load those two customers into the data warehouse.

00:48.640 --> 00:51.800
So in the data warehouse we will get as well one and two.

00:51.840 --> 00:53.080
So this is for the first day.

00:53.120 --> 00:54.040
Nothing is crazy.

00:54.080 --> 00:56.240
We just load the data as it is now.

00:56.240 --> 01:00.660
For the second day we will get a new data from the source system and it's going to look like this.

01:00.660 --> 01:04.860
So now if you check the second day you can see that we have again the customer number one, we have

01:04.860 --> 01:06.660
already loaded to the data warehouse.

01:06.940 --> 01:11.860
So we have it as the previous day, but we have a new customer customer ID number three.

01:11.860 --> 01:17.740
So now in order to load only the new data we don't need to load again the customer number one, what

01:17.740 --> 01:24.020
we can do, we can do an accept between the day number two with the previous load with the day number

01:24.020 --> 01:24.300
one.

01:24.300 --> 01:30.700
So now if we simply do an accept between those two sets, we're going to go and identify the new data

01:30.700 --> 01:34.780
that is existing in the source system which is only the record number three.

01:34.780 --> 01:40.180
So now what can happen if we do accept between day two and day one, we will get one record, the new

01:40.180 --> 01:43.940
record that we're going to go and insert it inside our data warehouse.

01:43.940 --> 01:49.380
So as you can see, this set operator accept is very powerful in order to compare two sets, and not

01:49.380 --> 01:53.980
only for that analysis, we can use it, as you can see, for data engineering, in order to identify

01:54.220 --> 02:00.310
what is the new data that is generated from the sources in order to insert it inside our data warehouse.

02:04.390 --> 02:09.430
Okay, one more use case for the set operators that I personally use a lot in my project is that if

02:09.430 --> 02:14.830
you are doing data migrations, you can use the accept in order to check the data quality.

02:15.030 --> 02:18.870
And more specifically, we can use it in order to check the data completeness.

02:18.910 --> 02:19.110
Okay.

02:19.150 --> 02:23.190
So we have the following scenario where we are doing data migrations between two databases.

02:23.310 --> 02:27.230
So let's say that we would like to move this table from database A to database B.

02:27.230 --> 02:31.350
So we're going to go and load the table to the new database.

02:31.350 --> 02:36.990
And now what's very important after you move the data is that to check whether all the records did move

02:37.030 --> 02:41.470
from database A to database B, we are not missing anything even one record.

02:41.470 --> 02:43.550
So we want to do data completeness test.

02:43.550 --> 02:46.030
And there are many methods on how to do this test.

02:46.230 --> 02:48.630
One of them is to use that set operator.

02:48.630 --> 02:49.270
Except.

02:49.270 --> 02:50.030
So how are we going to do it.

02:50.070 --> 02:55.430
We're going to do an accept between the table from database A and the table from database B in order

02:55.430 --> 03:01.370
to find any records that is still in database A, which is not migrated to the database.

03:01.410 --> 03:05.290
B and of course, the best result is that we will not get anything.

03:05.410 --> 03:07.570
The result should be empty if we get an empty.

03:07.610 --> 03:12.290
That means all the rows from database A exist in the database B.

03:12.330 --> 03:13.970
And now of course we are not done yet.

03:14.010 --> 03:20.450
We want to do the comparison, but the way around we want to find any new rows that is in database B

03:20.650 --> 03:22.650
that we don't find in database A.

03:22.930 --> 03:25.250
Those two tables must be identical.

03:25.250 --> 03:27.450
So now what we're going to do, we're going to do an accept.

03:27.450 --> 03:30.130
But the first table is going to be from the database B.

03:30.170 --> 03:32.210
And then we're going to compare it with the database A.

03:32.250 --> 03:34.170
And we have the same expectation.

03:34.170 --> 03:36.130
The output should be as well empty.

03:36.170 --> 03:40.690
And now after doing the accepts twice for both sides and we are getting empty.

03:40.730 --> 03:46.370
And the results, that means those two tables are identical and we are not missing anything.

03:46.370 --> 03:51.890
So this is another amazing use case for the set operators in order to improve the quality of your data

03:51.890 --> 03:55.250
migrations and in order to do data completeness test.
